<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<androidx.coordinatorlayout.widget.CoordinatorLayout xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:app="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res-auto"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools"
    android:id="@+id/coordinatorLayout"
    android:layout_width="match_parent"
    android:layout_height="match_parent"
    android:fitsSystemWindows="true">

    <!-- ⚠️ NON-COLLAPSING BY DESIGN (2026-09-05). Do not reintroduce a CollapsingToolbarLayout here.
         WHY: the fast-scroll rail is drawn into the RecyclerView's ViewGroupOverlay (FastScroller's
         constructor calls mView.getOverlay().add(...) three times), so the rail can only ever span the
         RecyclerView. Under a CollapsingToolbarLayout, HeaderScrollingViewBehavior sized the RecyclerView
         to `viewport - collapsedHeight` and positioned it BELOW the header, so the rail began ~633px down
         the screen and the thumb rested there at the top of its own track. Four models were built hunting
         a displaced thumb before a rest: capture showed offset=0 padTop=0 thumbFrac=0.000 — it had always
         been at the top of a track that started too low. Drawing the rail higher dies on touch delivery:
         a RecyclerView.OnItemTouchListener only sees events delivered to the RecyclerView.
         With no `scroll` flag on any child, AppBarLayout.getTotalScrollRange() breaks its loop at i=0 and
         returns Math.max(0, 0) = 0 (decoded from the 1.14.0 AAR), so PixelFastScrollViewHelper's composite
         terms are both 0 and the arithmetic reduces to the flat-screen path exactly — the same reduction
         Home already gets. The cover moved into MediaHeaderAdapter (item 0); the
         TITLE did not follow it there - it is set on the toolbar instead (MediaFragment,
         binding.toolBar.title).
         DELIBERATELY GIVEN UP: the pinned collapsed title and the cover parallax/fade.

         paddingBottom="49dp" IS NOT MISSING — it was DELETED, not lost. It was residue of the CTL's
         circular measurement dependency (AppBarLayout and CTL both wrap_content, CTL height scaling ~1.5x
         paddingBottom rather than 1:1, cover size and expanded-title space contending through one knob),
         settled empirically at 43dp on 2026-06-15 after three uiautomator dumps and later carried at 49dp.
         With the CTL gone none of those inputs exist and the value has no justification HERE.
         (!) BUT IT WAS DOING A SECOND JOB, AND THIS NOTE ORIGINALLY MISSED IT (corrected 2026-09-06): the
         padding sat below the cover, so its visual effect was the cover-to-buttons gap, which is a design
         element and outlived the CTL. Deleting it flattened that gap to 0 and was reported from device as
         a regression on artist, album and playlist. The gap is now rebuilt where it belongs - as
         paddingBottom on the cover wrapper in item_media_header.xml, at 49dp + the RecyclerView's 8dp
         paddingTop = 57dp, since that 8dp now falls above the cover instead of below it. Read the note
         there before touching either number. What stays true is the narrow claim: do not restore 49dp TO
         THIS FILE, where nothing measures against it any more. The same applies to the 2026-06-13 title work — titleCollapseMode fade,
         maxLines 2, expandedTitleMarginEnd, and the expandedTitleEllipsize attribute that does not exist in
         Material 1.14.0 — all CTL attributes with no successor here. -->
    <!-- ⚠⚠ TRIED AND REVERTED 2026-09-07 — DO NOT RE-PROPOSE THE OVERLAP AS AN OBVIOUS WIN. ⚠⚠
         For one build (1085) the FragmentContainerView carried
         app:layout_behavior="dev.brahmkshatriya.echo.utils.ui.OverlapScrollingViewBehavior" instead of
         @string/appbar_scrolling_view_behavior, so the list spanned the full viewport and the artwork
         scrolled under a transparent, scrimmed toolbar. The goal was real and narrow: the fast-scroll rail
         is drawn into the RecyclerView's own overlay, so a RecyclerView that starts below the toolbar has
         a rail that starts below the toolbar, and the thumb sits in a strip the user cannot reach.
         IT PRODUCED FIVE DEFECTS AT ONCE, all reported from device on album and artist:
           1. The cover was CLIPPED AT THE TOP — its upper edge ran under the status bar.
           2. appbarOutline drew a HORIZONTAL LINE ACROSS THE MIDDLE OF THE ARTWORK (it is anchored to the
              AppBarLayout's bottom, which now fell inside the cover instead of above it).
           3. The scrim DID NOT VISIBLY DARKEN ANYTHING — back arrow and overflow floated over bright art.
           4. Mid-scroll the title rendered TWICE AND OVERLAPPING, once in the header content and once in
              the toolbar: the scroll-driven toolbar fade never worked and the toolbar title was solid the
              whole time.
           5. The thumb RESTED BEHIND THE TOOLBAR and could not be grabbed — the exact problem the overlap
              existed to solve, reintroduced one layer up.
         The rail starting below the toolbar strip — what this was meant to fix — is a SMALLER problem than
         any single one of those.
         ⚠️ WHY IT IS NOT A ONE-ATTRIBUTE WIN: the overlap needs the outline anchor, the scrim, the
         scroller padding, the cover's top inset and the title fade ALL SOLVED TOGETHER. Each is load-bearing
         and none of them was solved. Anyone reviving this should treat it as five pieces of work with one
         switch, not as a layout_behavior swap — and should start from the fact that symptom 5 means the
         motivating benefit did not even materialise.
         KEPT FROM THAT WORK, because none of it was implicated: the media-header migration itself, the
         title in the header content, and the 4dp/22dp spacing in item_media_header.xml.
         REMOVED WITH IT: OverlapScrollingViewBehavior (class deleted, plus its -keep rule in
         proguard-rules.pro and its anchor in verifyExtensionAbi's `critical` list, 15 anchors -> 14),
         android:background="@drawable/bg_toolbar_scrim" on the AppBarLayout below, liftOnScroll="false",
         stateListAnimator="@null", the RecyclerView top padding and MediaDetailsFragment.setupToolbarFade.
         The scrim, the lift suppression and the declaration order below existed ONLY to make the overlap
         readable/touchable; with the toolbar opaque and above the content again they have no job, and the
         drawable @drawable/bg_toolbar_scrim is left in the tree unreferenced for whoever tries this next.
         ⚠️ AND THE DECLARATION ORDER IS PART OF THE REVERT. For that one build the FragmentContainerView
         was declared FIRST so the AppBarLayout would win CoordinatorLayout's reverse-order hit test.
         Restoring it to LAST also restores TV initial focus to the toolbar's navigation icon
         (ViewGroup.onRequestFocusInDescendants walks children in RAW INDEX ORDER, unlike directional
         D-pad and FOCUS_FORWARD which are geometric and were never affected). That is the pre-1085
         behaviour; the note claiming first focus should land on a button described the overlap build. -->
    <com.google.android.material.appbar.AppBarLayout
        android:id="@+id/appBarLayout"
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
        android:fitsSystemWindows="true">

        <com.google.android.material.appbar.MaterialToolbar
            android:id="@+id/toolBar"
            style="@style/Toolbar"
            android:background="@android:color/transparent"
            app:menu="@menu/more_menu"
            tools:title="@tools:sample/full_names" />

    </com.google.android.material.appbar.AppBarLayout>

    <View
        android:id="@+id/appbarOutline"
        style="@style/AppBarOutline"
        app:layout_anchor="@id/appBarLayout"
        app:layout_anchorGravity="bottom" />

    <androidx.fragment.app.FragmentContainerView
        android:id="@+id/mediaFragmentContainer"
        android:name="dev.brahmkshatriya.echo.ui.media.MediaDetailsFragment"
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="match_parent"
        app:layout_behavior="@string/appbar_scrolling_view_behavior"
        tools:layout="@layout/fragment_media_details" />

</androidx.coordinatorlayout.widget.CoordinatorLayout>